= Purpose and the paper being backed

This document backs the draft paper by J. P. Palacios et al., _"Convexification Strategies for Optimal Power Flow in Coupled Transmission and Distribution Networks"_ (draft of 2026-09-29), Sections VII and VIII. The paper builds a hybrid convex relaxation of a 6-node transmission-and-distribution (T&D) system. The meshed transmission nodes 1-3 are a $3 times 3$ Hermitian positive semidefinite block $W$ (SDP). The radial feeder 3-4-5-6 is the DistFlow model with its second-order-cone (SOC) relaxation. The two parts are coupled at the boundary bus 3 through $W_(33) = v_3$. The paper then decomposes the problem between the TSO and the DSO with a Gauss-Seidel ADMM.

The case study has five parts:

+ *Experiment 0, baseline self-check.* Reproduce the paper's monolithic optimum and stop with an error if any published number is missed.
+ *Experiment 1, global optimality.* Solve the non-convex AC-OPF with Ipopt from many starts. This tests the claim that the relaxed solution is the global AC optimum.
+ *Experiment 2, ADMM.* Re-run the paper's ADMM and stress its two guidance statements: "$rho$ on the scale of the prices" and "$epsilon$ small or zero".
+ *Experiment 3, congestion.* The paper leaves this for future work.
+ *Experiment 4, inexact regimes.* Also left for future work by the paper. Includes the effect and cost of the LinDistFlow exactness copy.

Disagreements with the paper are reported as found. The experiments were not tuned to make the paper look right.

= System, model and implementation

== Data and conventions

The data are those of the paper's Table I. $S_"base" = 100$ MVA. Transmission lines (1,2), (2,3), (1,3) use the $pi$ model with total charging $b$ and $S^max = 2.0$ pu. Feeder branches (3,4), (4,5), (5,6) have $S^max = I^max = 1.0, 0.6, 0.4$ pu. Loads are $P_d = (0, 0.90, 0.60, 0.12, 0.10, 0.08)$ and $Q_d = (0, 0.30, 0.20, 0.05, 0.04, 0.03)$ pu. Generation cost is $1100 P_g^2 + 2000 P_g + 100$ \$/h with $0 <= P_g <= 3$ and $-1.5 <= Q_g <= 1.5$. All $|V| in [0.95, 1.05]$. Prices are balance duals in \$/h per pu divided by 100, i.e. \$/MWh (\$/Mvarh). Nodes 1-3 are priced from the SDP balances, nodes 4-6 from the DistFlow balances.

== What is reused from the framework and what is new

The model builders never name a solver. Clarabel (SOCP/SDP) and Ipopt (NLP) come from the framework's solver factory `select_optimizer`. The following are framework pieces:

- the validated radial `Feeder` for nodes 3-6;
- the "framework-hosted" DSO block built by `contribute!(ConvexBranchFlow(), ...)` on the same JuMP model as the SDP block, with `add_to_residual!` and `close_balance!` for the balances;
- the exactness gate `assert_socp_exact!` and its hybrid floor $max(tau, epsilon dot S_max^2)$ with $tau = 2 times 10^(-7)$, $epsilon = 10^(-9)$ and $"rtol" = 10^(-4)$;
- the feeder DLMP decomposition `decompose_dlmp`;
- the Baran-Wu phasor recovery `recover_voltage_angles`.

Four pieces are new and live in the script:

- the transmission SDP block (paper eqs. 20-28);
- a paper-faithful DistFlow block (eqs. 29-33) with an optional mirror of the framework's exactness copy;
- the TSO and DSO ADMM subproblems (eqs. 35-42). They are built once; each iteration only resets the objective;
- the polar AC-OPF of the full 6-node system.

*Framework finding.* `ConvexBranchFlow` fixes the feeder-root squared voltage $v$ and its copy $hat(v)$ at 1.0. It therefore cannot represent a T&D boundary where $v_3 = W_(33)$ is a decision variable. The script works around this without changing the framework:

+ after `contribute!`, unfix the root $v$ and $hat(v)$;
+ re-impose the squared-voltage bounds on the root;
+ add $v_"root" = "Re"(W_(33))$ and $hat(v)_"root" = v_"root"$;
+ inject the boundary import, which is shared with the SDP node-3 balance, into the residuals.

The framework block differs from the paper model in three ways:

- it adds a receiving-end apparent-power cone on every limited branch, where the paper has only the sending end;
- it always carries the exactness copy;
- it has no current limit $l <= I_max^2$, so the script adds one.

At the baseline the two models agree to $9.85 times 10^(-7)$ \$/h in cost and $2.4 times 10^(-5)$ \$/MWh in prices. `assert_socp_exact!` passes (max $|l v - P^2 - Q^2| = 7.0 times 10^(-11)$), and the framework's phasor recovery matches the script's recursion to $5.9 times 10^(-11)$ pu.

*Solver setting (measured).* With Clarabel's default equilibration, the monolithic solve stops at the same iterate for every tolerance from $10^(-7)$ to $10^(-9)$. The status is OPTIMAL at $10^(-7)$ and ALMOST_OPTIMAL below. The cone residual on (3,4) is $1.1 times 10^(-7)$, and the balance duals deviate from finite differences by up to $7 times 10^(-4)$ \$/MWh. Disabling equilibration reaches OPTIMAL at $10^(-9)$, and the duals then agree with finite differences. The script therefore uses this setting throughout. There is one exception: if a solve ends in a numerical failure, it is repeated once with equilibration on, and the result records which setting was used.

