= Experiment 1: global optimality

Ipopt ($"tol" = 10^(-10)$) solved the polar AC-OPF of the full 6-node system from 26 starts:

- a flat start;
- 20 seeded random starts with $|V| tilde U[0.95, 1.05]$, $theta tilde U[-0.3, 0.3]$ rad and $P_g, Q_g$ uniform within their bounds;
- the framework's 5 deterministic Ipopt strategy variants.

The AC model uses the relaxation's limits:

- transmission $|S|$ limits at both line ends;
- feeder sending-end $|S|$ limits;
- the current limit $|S_(i j)|^2 / |V_i|^2 <= I_max^2$.

*All 26 starts were locally solved and reached a single optimum*, 7461.105178 \$/h. The relaxation's cost is 7461.105181 \$/h, so the gap (AC − relaxation)/AC is $-3.98 times 10^(-10)$. The relaxation is above the AC value by $3 times 10^(-6)$ \$/h. This is below Clarabel's $10^(-9)$ relative accuracy, so the gap is zero within solver tolerance.

The relaxed point is also AC feasible:

- the voltages recovered from $W$'s leading eigenvector, extended down the feeder by the Baran-Wu recursion, differ from the Ipopt optimum by at most $1.25 times 10^(-8)$ pu (complex);
- substituted into the full 6-node admittance matrix, they leave AC injection mismatches of $1.28 times 10^(-10)$ pu (P) and $7.87 times 10^(-10)$ pu (Q);
- the recovered voltages are $V_4 = 1.00354∠−6.923°$, $V_5 = 0.98454∠−7.225°$ and $V_6 = 0.97500∠−7.398°$.

This confirms with an independent non-convex solver the paper's claim that the relaxed solution is the global AC-OPF optimum on this case.

= Experiment 2: TSO-DSO ADMM

The paper's Gauss-Seidel ADMM was re-implemented as specified (eqs. 35-42):

- $rho = 500$, $epsilon = 10^(-2)$;
- $lambda^((0)) = 0$ and $y_D^((0)) = (1, sum P_d, sum Q_d)$;
- stop when the primal residual $||y_T - y_D||_2 < 10^(-4)$ pu and the dual residual $rho ||Delta y_D||_2 < 10^(-2)$.

All subproblem solves ended OPTIMAL.

