== Loss-penalty sweep

For $epsilon in {0, 10^(-4), 10^(-3), 10^(-2), 10^(-1)}$ at $rho = 500$:

- every run converged in *57 iterations* with the same final cost bias ($-7.61 times 10^(-5)$);
- the largest cone residual over *all intermediate DSO iterates* was between $1.55 times 10^(-9)$ and $2.66 times 10^(-9)$; the largest hybrid-floor ratio over the iterates was $2.46 times 10^(-3)$, far below 1;
- at $epsilon = 0$ the maximum over all iterates was $1.82 times 10^(-9)$, and $5.98 times 10^(-10)$ at convergence.

The paper's statement that "$epsilon$ can be kept small (or set to zero)" therefore *holds on this case*. The DSO subproblem never used the slack in its cones, even in intermediate iterates. The reason is the one the paper gives: the boundary multiplier carries the TSO's positive marginal cost into the DSO objective.

The incentive depends on that price being positive. Experiment 4 shows that when the system is in surplus, the generator sits at $P_g = 0$ and the monolithic relaxation itself loses exactness. The $epsilon = 0$ result should therefore be stated as conditional on a positive boundary price.

#figure(resfig("admm_eps_sweep", width: 60%), caption: [Cone residual vs $epsilon$: maximum over all DSO iterates and at convergence; the dashed line is the framework's solver floor $tau = 2 times 10^(-7)$.])

= Experiment 3: congestion

== The paper's system cannot be congested meaningfully

The planned test was to tighten line (1,2) and the feeder head (3,4) to 0.9 times their baseline flows, i.e. $S^max_(12) = 1.02427$ and $S^max_(34) = I^max_(34) = 0.29802$ pu. *Both are infeasible.* The relaxation is infeasible, which certifies AC infeasibility because it is a relaxation, and Ipopt from 21 starts finds no AC point.

The reason is structural: with one generator and inelastic loads nothing can be redispatched. The flows are fixed by the loads, and only the voltage profile can move them slightly. Minimizing each flow subject to all other constraints gives the band in which such a limit can bind at all:

- line (1,2): $max|S_(12)|$ can be reduced to 1.137122 pu in the relaxation but *not at all* in AC (Ipopt minimum 1.138073 = baseline);
- feeder head: the minimum $|S_(34)|$ equals the baseline 0.331135 pu in both models.

Inside these bands two pathologies appear:

- at the line band midpoint ($S^max_(12) = 1.137598$) the relaxation is feasible but *not rank one* ($|lambda_2/lambda_1| = 1.45 times 10^(-4)$), Ipopt finds no AC point, prices jump to 244 \$/MWh at node 2, and ADMM does not converge in 3000 iterations;
- at the feeder-head band ($S^max_(34) = 0.331135$) the limit binds at a degenerate point and its dual is *not unique*: the same optimum is priced at node 4 as 187.17 \$/MWh by the paper model, 96.77 \$/MWh by the framework-hosted model, and close to the uncongested value by ADMM (spread 132 \$/MWh).

== Congestion with out-of-merit flexibility (extension, not in the paper)

To study congestion with a well-posed redispatch, two out-of-merit resources were added. Both are active-power only, and both are idle at the baseline:

- a generator G2 at node 2 with cost $1100 P_(g 2)^2 + 6500 P_(g 2)$ \$/h, $P_(g 2) <= 1$. Its marginal cost of 65 \$/MWh exceeds the baseline $pi_(P,2) = 61.88$;
- a DG at node 6 with cost $7000 P_"dg"$ \$/h, $P_"dg" <= 0.2$. Its 70 \$/MWh exceeds the baseline $pi_(P,6) = 65.94$.

The script checks that this extension leaves the baseline unchanged: $|Delta "cost"| = 6.1 times 10^(-5)$ \$/h, max $|Delta pi| = 1.9 times 10^(-4)$ \$/MWh. A first version also gave G2 a reactive range. Even at $P_(g 2) = 0$ that range lowered the baseline cost by 7.4 \$/h through voltage support, so it was not out of merit and was removed. The planned 0.9× limits then apply as specified.

