*With the paper's ratings (stages A and B) there is no inexact regime.* At all 52 feasible points the relaxation is exact in both blocks and matches the AC optimum within $3.13 times 10^(-9)$. It becomes infeasible at exactly the PV level where Ipopt stops finding AC points, on the 0.05 pu grid. The PV limits are 0.45 pu at node 6 and 0.80 / 0.70 pu at node 5. Two features of this regime matter. The feeder thermal and voltage limits cap the back-feed long before the generator could reach $P_g = 0$. The voltage upper bound at node 6 does bind (from PV 0.40 pu at 100 % load), yet the relaxation stays exact.

*With lifted feeder ratings (stage C) an inexact regime appears*, always beyond the AC-feasible range. Over 51 points the relaxation is feasible, but Ipopt finds no AC point at any of them, including with the deepened searches. In the AC model the voltage limits make these PV levels infeasible. The relaxation instead reports a feasible, priced operating point with large fictitious losses: cone residuals up to 4.81, 7.33 and 5.70 pu at 100 %, 50 % and 25 % load. Two mechanisms produce it:

- *Loads at 100 % and 50 %.* $|V_6|$ sits at the upper bound and another node at the lower bound. Inflating $l$ on (5,6) adds fictitious reactive absorption $x l$ and lowers $v_6$, which no AC point can do. The SDP block stays rank one ($|lambda_2/lambda_1| <= 6.7 times 10^(-11)$).
- *Loads at 25 %, the surplus case.* $P_g$ reaches its lower bound 0, the cost is the constant 100 \$/h, and the surplus is dissipated in fictitious losses. *Here the SDP block also loses rank one*: $|lambda_2/lambda_1|$ ranges from $4.2 times 10^(-4)$ to $4.6 times 10^(-3)$. This is the only regime in this study where the transmission SDP is inexact.

In every case the paper's a-posteriori tightness test (rank ratio and cone residuals) flags the point, so the test does its job. The practical risk is different: an unverified relaxation would report a *feasible operating point and prices where no AC solution exists*.

*The exactness copy does not cure this regime and is itself conservative.* Where Ipopt found an AC point, the copy costs more than the true AC optimum:

- by at most $8.0 times 10^(-5)$ to $5.0 times 10^(-4)$ relative with the paper's ratings;
- by up to *2.9 %* (2.92e-02 at PV 0.80, 100 % load) and 2.8 % (PV 0.70, 50 % load) with lifted ratings. At those two AC-feasible points the copy model is moreover inexact itself: it is pushed into a slack-cone solution while the relaxation without copy is exact and equal to the AC optimum.

Beyond the AC limit the copy model stays feasible for a range of PV levels and is inexact throughout. It is feasible up to 1.15 pu PV (vs 1.40 without copy) at 100 % load and up to 1.10 pu at 50 % and 25 % load. The copy narrows the falsely feasible range but does not remove it.

The framework-hosted model (copy plus receiving-end cone) agrees with the script's copy model to $<= 3.3 times 10^(-5)$ \$/h in every sweep except B at 50 % load. There it differs by up to 26.2 \$/h because its receiving-end apparent-power limit binds under back-feed, and the paper's model has no such limit.

