= Recommendations for the paper text

*Claims the paper can now make, with these numbers:*

+ The monolithic optimum and all of Table III are independently reproduced (JuMP + Clarabel). The cost is 7461.1052 \$/h, and all 12 prices are within $4.7 times 10^(-4)$ \$/MWh of Table III and validated by finite differences to $3.2 times 10^(-5)$. Suggest stating that the prices were checked against finite differences of the optimal cost with $h = 10^(-4)$ pu.
+ Global optimality is confirmed by a non-convex solver. Ipopt from 26 different starts reaches a single AC optimum, 7461.105178 \$/h, equal to the relaxation within $4 times 10^(-10)$. The voltages recovered from $W$ satisfy the full AC equations to $8 times 10^(-10)$ pu.
+ The ADMM results (57 iterations, primal residual $9.1 times 10^(-5)$, cost 7460.537 \$/h, prices settling after about 40 iterations, $-lambda_p = 6258.80$) are reproduced exactly.
+ "$rho$ on the scale of the prices" can be quantified: 523 iterations at $rho = 50$, 57 at $rho = 500$, and 8-11 at $rho in [5000, 20000]$, a range that brackets $pi_(P,3) dot 100 approx 6259$.
+ "$epsilon$ small or zero" can be quantified: with $epsilon = 0$ the cone residual over all intermediate iterates stays $<= 1.8 times 10^(-9)$, and iterations and final bias do not change.

*Statements to soften or reword:*

+ The specific cone residuals (2.6e-7, 2.9e-8, 2.3e-8) depend on the solver's accuracy. The same model gives $4.7 times 10^(-10)$ and below with a more accurate Clarabel setting. Say "below $10^(-6)$, i.e. zero within solver tolerance" and report the solver settings, which matter: Clarabel's default equilibration stops at ALMOST_OPTIMAL with dual errors of order $10^(-3)$ \$/MWh.
+ The final dual residual ($1.8 times 10^(-3)$) did not reproduce ($1.02 times 10^(-3)$ here) although every other ADMM number did. Check its definition or norm in the text.
+ $rho = 500$ is about ten times smaller than the fastest setting. Either use $rho approx pi_(P,3) dot 100$ in the experiment, or state that $rho = 500$ is conservative and give the sweep.
+ The $epsilon = 0$ statement relies on a positive boundary price. Qualify it: in a surplus regime the incentive vanishes, and the coupled relaxation itself becomes inexact (Section 7).

*Future-work sentences that can become results:*

+ Congestion. On the paper's single-generator, inelastic-load system, congestion cannot be studied as such. A 10 % tighter limit on line (1,2) or on the feeder head is infeasible, and the feasible band is degenerate: it is empty in AC for line (1,2). Within it the SDP block loses rank one or the duals are not unique. With minimal out-of-merit flexibility (a generator at node 2, a DG at node 6), both relaxations stay exact under transmission, feeder-head and combined congestion. The AC gap is $<= 2 times 10^(-9)$, ADMM converges in 45-57 iterations, $-lambda_p = pi_(P,3) dot 100$ still holds, and the feeder DLMPs split into energy, loss, congestion and voltage components that sum to the nodal price within $1.4 times 10^(-14)$.
+ Inexactness. With the paper's ratings, PV back-feed at node 5 or 6 never makes the relaxation inexact; its infeasibility coincides with AC infeasibility. With lifted feeder ratings, a regime appears beyond the AC hosting limit in which the relaxation reports a feasible, priced point with fictitious losses (cone residuals up to 7.3 pu). In surplus conditions ($P_g = 0$) the SDP block also loses rank one ($|lambda_2/lambda_1|$ up to $4.6 times 10^(-3)$). The a-posteriori test flags every such point, so the paper's verification step is necessary, not optional. The LinDistFlow exactness copy does not cure this regime: it costs up to 2.9 % more than the AC optimum at AC-feasible points and can itself be inexact there.
